\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The top exterior word is the type-B spin highest vector}

\p{Let
#{V=W\oplus W'\oplus\mathbb C z} be a nondegenerate quadratic space of
dimension #{2\ell+1}, with #{\ell\geq 1}. Assume #{W,W'} are complementary
polar-dual isotropic spaces, #{z} is orthogonal to both, and #{Q(z)=1}.
Choose bases #{e_i,e_i'} with #{B_Q(e_i,e_j')=\delta_{ij}} and the plus odd
action on #{S=\bigwedge W},

##{\rho(\iota_Q(z))=\alpha,}

where #{\alpha} is exterior parity. This sign is part of the choice:
#{Q(z)=1} alone says only that the line coordinate squares to #{1}. Write
#{c_i,a_i,N_i,H_i,\epsilon_i} as in \ref{fcap-0025}.}

\p{Choose the positive roots

##{\Phi^+=\{\epsilon_i-\epsilon_j,\ \epsilon_i+\epsilon_j:i<j\}
\cup\{\epsilon_i:1\leq i\leq\ell\}.}

The chain simple roots use #{E_i=c_i a_{i+1}} and
#{F_i=c_{i+1}a_i}. At the terminal short root
#{\alpha_\ell=\epsilon_\ell}, take

##{E_\ell=c_\ell\alpha,\qquad
F_\ell=\alpha a_\ell,\qquad
[E_\ell,F_\ell]=2N_\ell-1.}

The matrix short root has a coefficient #{2} because
#{B_Q(z,z)=2}; the Clifford bivector is nevertheless exactly
#{e_\ell z}, with no extra factor #{\frac12}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeBQuadraticEquiv_typeBShortRootGenerator}.
On an increasing word, the terminal creation or removal sign cancels the
parity sign, so the short operators insert or remove coordinate #{\ell}
with coefficient #{1}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeBSpinRep_shortRootGenerator_apply}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeBSpinRep_shortNegativeRootGenerator_apply}.}

\p{This convention gives the same flag Borel as in
\ref{fcap-002X}. For #{i<j}, put
#{X_{ij}=c_i a_j}, #{Y_{ij}=c_i c_j}, and #{K_i=c_i\alpha}. The chain
commutators generate every #{X_{ij}}, while

##{[X_{ij},K_j]=K_i,\qquad [K_i,K_j]=-2Y_{ij}.}

Because #{2} is invertible over #{\mathbb C}, the simple chain operators
and #{K_\ell} generate the root spaces of every positive short and long
root. They therefore generate the full positive nilradical.}

\p{The top word

##{\Omega_B=e_1\wedge\cdots\wedge e_\ell}

is killed by each positive chain root and by #{E_\ell}, since the relevant
creation coordinate is already occupied. The chain coroots have
eigenvalue #{0}, and the terminal coroot #{2N_\ell-1} has eigenvalue
#{1}. Hence

##{\operatorname{wt}(\Omega_B)
=\omega_\ell=\tfrac12(\epsilon_1+\cdots+\epsilon_\ell).}

The chain moves together with the terminal toggle connect all exterior
basis words. As in \ref{fcap-002Y}, the distinct joint #{H_i}-weights
first project any nonzero invariant subspace onto a basis line; the root
moves then reach every line. Thus #{S} is the irreducible type-#{B_\ell}
module #{L(\omega_\ell)}. The short root changes exterior degree, so
neither #{S^+} nor #{S^-} is a type-B submodule.}

\p{\citet{Proposition 20.20, p. 307}{fulton2004representation}
independently identifies the odd-dimensional spin representation as the
irreducible module of highest weight
#{\frac12(\epsilon_1+\cdots+\epsilon_\ell)}. The preceding short-root and
nilradical calculations give that conclusion in the fixed FCAP operator
convention.}

\p{\citet{Proposition 6.2.4, p. 315}{goodman2009symmetry}
independently gives the same irreducible type-B module and its half-sign
weights. As in \ref{fcap-0025}, its occupied Cartan sign is opposite ours;
negating its Cartan axes and positive system gives the displayed highest
weight. TauCeti formalizes the short and long root actions and the
integral simple-coroot eigenvalues
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeBSpinRep_longRootGenerator_apply}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.spinAction_typeBQuadraticEquiv_typeBSimpleCorootGenerator_basis},
but the highest-vector, Borel-generation, and irreducibility conclusion
here is the displayed mathematical synthesis rather than a whole-card
formal theorem.}
